lazysite-apache for Debian
==========================

Plain-Apache glue for lazysite (the SM139 glue packages): the shipped
vhost templates at /usr/share/lazysite-apache/ and the
lazysite-apache-vhost command that renders them into
/etc/apache2/sites-available/. It never touches site content, and it
prints - never runs - the a2enmod/a2ensite/reload steps, so the
host-wide actions stay visible operator choices.

Two patterns (one vhost file each):

  vhost-cgi   Plain CGI. Zero extra moving parts: static files are
              served directly, page misses fall through FallbackResource
              to the processor via the CGI auth wrapper. Every page
              render forks a CGI - the simplest correct starting point
              on Apache.
  vhost-fcgi  FastCGI pool. Anonymous page traffic proxies to the
              persistent per-site lazysite@<domain> pool socket
              (~150x faster on cache hits); session-bearing traffic,
              /manager, /cgi-bin and /dav stay on the CGI path (the
              pool is anonymous by design). The production shape.

Which pattern: start with vhost-cgi; move to vhost-fcgi when the site
matters. Switching is `lazysite-apache-vhost add <domain> --fcgi --force`
plus the pool setup below - site content is untouched.

Per-domain wiring (3 steps)
---------------------------

1. Provision the site AS ITS USER (never root - see lazysite(1)):

       sudo -u siteuser lazysite provision \
           --docroot /srv/www/example.com/public_html \
           --cgibin  /srv/www/example.com/cgi-bin \
           --domain  example.com

2. Render the vhost (as root; --cgibin defaults to the cgi-bin sibling
   of the docroot):

       lazysite-apache-vhost add example.com \
           --docroot /srv/www/example.com/public_html            # CGI
       lazysite-apache-vhost add example.com --fcgi \
           --docroot /srv/www/example.com/public_html            # pool

3. Run the printed steps: a2enmod (cgid headers rewrite, plus proxy
   proxy_fcgi for --fcgi), a2ensite, configtest, reload. HTTPS:
   `certbot --apache -d example.com` derives the SSL vhost.

For --fcgi the pool must exist before pages render:
/etc/lazysite/pools/example.com.conf + `systemctl enable --now
lazysite@example.com` - the pattern is documented in the
lazysite-common README.Debian and in the unit file.

`lazysite-apache-vhost remove <domain>` deletes the vhost file only -
site content is never touched.

Trying lazysite without a web server: `lazysite demo` (lazysite-common)
provisions a scratch site and serves it on the built-in dev server.

The full multi-server reference (these patterns explained once, plus
nginx, Caddy, lighttpd and the generic front-end contract) is
/usr/share/doc/lazysite-apache/webserver-wiring.md.

